  "answer_text": "**Absolutely \u2014 what we eat matters enormously!** Here's a breakdown of why:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udd66 Food is the Foundation of Health\n\nThe food we eat gives our bodies the \"information\" and materials they need to function properly. If we don't get the right information, our metabolic processes suffer and our health declines. If we get too much food, or food that gives our bodies the wrong instructions, we can become overweight, undernourished, and at risk for diseases such as arthritis, diabetes, and heart disease.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf4e Quality Matters, Not Just Calories\n\nWhen it comes to staying healthy, it may not be enough to just count calories \u2014 what you choose to eat really does matter. An overall healthy diet containing plenty of fruits, vegetables, and whole grains can help prevent weight gain.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Diet and Chronic Disease\n\nDiet plays a critical role in shaping the health and well-being of both individuals and populations, and unhealthy diets are a major risk factor for disease and disability.\n\nResearch shows that a diet rich in red meat and processed meat leads to a high risk of cancer, especially colorectal cancer. Substantial evidence also indicates that eating more vegetables and fruits and less meat is able to decrease overall cancer risk and improve health.\n\nHealthy eating can lower your risk of chronic health conditions, such as heart disease, type 2 diabetes, and some cancers.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udde0 Diet and Mental Health\n\nEating a poor diet reduces physical and mental health, because eating healthy allows people to be more active. Two-thirds of people who eat fresh fruit and vegetables daily report no mental health issues. Compare this to those who do have some level of mental health problems, who have generally reported eating less healthy foods and more unhealthy foods.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf0d Diet and the Bigger Picture\n\nNutrition involves our relationships with family, friends, community, the environment, and the world. We need to make decisions about what and how we eat that foster not only our health and wellbeing, but the health of those around us and of our planet.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 The Bottom Line\n\nTrendy diets and nutrition research change almost daily. Still, amidst all the hype, study after study shows that good food choices have a positive impact on health, and poor diets have negative long-term effects.\n\nIn short, what we eat is one of the most powerful tools we have for maintaining our health, preventing disease, and feeling our best!",
